 s takes Kenneth Clark’s landmark 1938 book One Hundred Details from Pict
 ures in the National Gallery as a starting point to illuminate the impact 
 black and white photographic details of Old Master paintings had on new wa
 ys of seeing in the early 20th century. The exhibition reveals the ana\n\n
 Ages: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-09-24 10:45 PDT
LOCATION:McMurtry Building
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Stanford] World Development Report 2026: The Promise of Artificia
 l Intelligence
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260924T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260924T161500
DTSTAMP:20260924T174557Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53878832958189-53878832960238@local-events
DESCRIPTION:In a time when development progress has dipped to its weakest 
 pace in 75 years\, artificial intelligence (AI) offers developing economie
 s a rare path to greater prosperity. World Development Report 2026 provide
 s the first comprehensive assessment of what AI means for these economies 
 — how firms and governments are already using it\, what is holding them 
 back\, where the largest gains may lie\, and how the risks can be managed.
 \n\nAges: All ages\nSource: https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/events/world-deve
 lopment-report-2026-promise-artificial-intelligence\nLast synced: 2026-09-
 24 10:45 PDT
LOCATION:Vidalakis Dining Hall
URL:https://www.gsb.stanford.edu/events/world-development-report-2026-prom
 ise-artificial-intelligence
END:VEVENT
