BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Center on Democracy\, Development and the Rule of Law] Emily Russ
 ell — Policing the Plantation: Repressive Institutions and Labor Control
  in Assam\, India
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260924T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260924T131500
DTSTAMP:20260924T174557Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-54003623062704-54003623063729@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Policing is a mainstay of modern states\, yet police abuses pe
 rsist even in democracies. This article advances a new explanation for rep
 ression\, arguing that police impose not only social order\, but also econ
 omic order in developing economies. This model\, called labor repressive p
 olicing\, theorizes that landowners mobilize police when they have incenti
 ves to control workers but the costs of private coercion are high. I study
  this process through a mixed-methods examination of the tea plantation e
 \n\nAges: All ages\nSource: https://cddrl.fsi.stanford.edu/events/emily-ru
 ssell-policing-plantation-repressive-institutions-and-labor-control-assam-
 india\nLast synced: 2026-09-24 10:45 PDT
URL:https://cddrl.fsi.stanford.edu/events/emily-russell-policing-plantatio
 n-repressive-institutions-and-labor-control-assam-india
END:VEVENT
